## Which files this covers

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `api/data/graph.json` | the full graph, for the Python gateway |
| `api/data/graph.campus` | the same graph in the compact format the C++ engine loads |

Both are produced by `pipeline/extract.py` and `pipeline/transform.py` from an
Overpass API download. They are a **Derivative Database** under the ODbL, not
merely a Produced Work, because they restructure and re-tag OpenStreetMap's own
data rather than only rendering a picture of it. That is why they carry the
ODbL rather than the MIT license the source code uses.

If you modify these files and distribute the result, the ODbL asks you to
publish the modified database under the ODbL as well.

## One local correction

`pipeline/patches.json` declares a small number of hand-checked corrections that
are applied on top of the OpenStreetMap download. Today that is a single 15.8 m
footway at Student Center East Tower, connecting an entrance node to a footpath
node, both of which already exist in OpenStreetMap. No geometry was traced or
invented, and a patch skips itself automatically if OpenStreetMap ever gains the
connection. See the README for why it is there.

## Everything else

Source code is MIT licensed, see LICENSE.

Map tiles are served by the OpenStreetMap Foundation and are not redistributed
here. Bundled dependencies keep their own licenses: most are MIT or BSD,
react-leaflet is Hippocratic 2.1, and the IBM Plex fonts are SIL OFL 1.1. See the
README for the list.
